@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+# Summary
+
+## Changed
+- `evlog/cli.py`: `report` takes `--since DATE` (`YYYY-MM-DD` or ISO 8601). Invalid values give an argparse error (exit 2), not a traceback. An unreadable file or a malformed event line also now gives a clean error (exit 2).
+- `evlog/events.py`: timestamps are normalised to aware UTC (naive = UTC, per the README), so aware and naive events compare correctly. `count_events` takes `since` and keeps events at or after it (inclusive).
+- `README.md`: documents `--since`.
+- `tests/test_since.py` (new): UTC comparison of offset events, inclusive boundary, naive = UTC, `--since` with an offset, bad date, missing file. No existing tests were touched.
+
+## Semantics
+A bare date means midnight UTC. Event offsets are honoured, so `2024-03-12T08:15:00+09:00` counts as 2024-03-11 23:15 UTC. Ops in other regions may expect local midnight; that is not supported.
+
+## Verified
+- `python -m unittest`: 7 tests, OK. This includes the teammate's `test_since_excludes_older_days`.
+- `python -m evlog report /dev/null --since 2024-13-01` printed a one-line usage error, exit 2, no traceback.
+- `python -m evlog report nofile` printed `cannot read nofile: No such file or directory`, exit 2.
+- My first run of `test_since_with_offset` failed because the expected value was wrong. I fixed the test, not the code.
+
+## Not verified
+- The malformed-event error path (bad JSON or a missing key) has no test. I did not run it by hand either.
+- I did not test the `--since` parsing with a `Z` suffix. That depends on the Python version (3.11+ accepts it).
